# Terraform Refactor: Dev/Prod Blocks to `for_each` Module

## Strategy

To achieve zero-diff state migration, you'll:

1. **Create a reusable module** parameterized by environment
2. **Migrate state** using `terraform state mv` (address change only, no resource recreation)
3. **Validate** with `terraform plan` showing no drift

---

## Step 1: Module Structure

Create `modules/app/main.tf`:

```hcl
locals {
  env_config = {
    dev = {
      cluster_node_count = 2
      cluster_node_type  = "t3.medium"
      rds_instance_class = "db.t3.micro"
      rds_multi_az       = false
      enable_replication = false
      enable_oidc        = false
    }
    prod = {
      cluster_node_count = 4
      cluster_node_type  = "t3.large"
      rds_instance_class = "db.t3.medium"
      rds_multi_az       = true
      enable_replication = true
      enable_oidc        = true
    }
  }

  config = local.env_config[var.environment]
}

# EKS Cluster
resource "aws_eks_cluster" "main" {
  name    = "${var.app_name}-${var.environment}"
  version = var.kubernetes_version

  role_arn = aws_iam_role.cluster.arn

  vpc_config {
    subnet_ids = var.subnet_ids
  }

  depends_on = [aws_iam_role_policy_attachment.cluster_policy]
}

resource "aws_eks_node_group" "main" {
  cluster_name    = aws_eks_cluster.main.name
  node_group_name = "${var.app_name}-${var.environment}-ng"
  node_role_arn   = aws_iam_role.node.arn
  subnet_ids      = var.subnet_ids
  version         = var.kubernetes_version

  scaling_config {
    desired_size = local.config.cluster_node_count
    max_size     = local.config.cluster_node_count + 2
    min_size     = 1
  }

  instance_types = [local.config.cluster_node_type]
}

# S3 Bucket
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "assets" {
  bucket = "${var.app_name}-assets-${var.environment}-${data.aws_caller_identity.current.account_id}"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_versioning" "assets" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.assets.id

  versioning_configuration {
    status = "Enabled"
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration" "assets" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.assets.id

  rule {
    apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
      sse_algorithm = "AES256"
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block" "assets" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.assets.id

  block_public_acls       = true
  block_public_policy     = true
  ignore_public_acls      = true
  restrict_public_buckets = true
}

# Conditional: prod replication to us-west-2
resource "aws_s3_bucket_replication_configuration" "assets" {
  count = local.config.enable_replication ? 1 : 0

  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.assets.id
  role   = aws_iam_role.s3_replication[0].arn

  depends_on = [aws_s3_bucket_versioning.assets]

  rule {
    status = "Enabled"

    destination {
      bucket       = aws_s3_bucket.assets_replica[0].arn
      storage_class = "STANDARD"
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "assets_replica" {
  count    = local.config.enable_replication ? 1 : 0
  provider = aws.west

  bucket = "${var.app_name}-assets-replica-${data.aws_caller_identity.current.account_id}"
}

# RDS PostgreSQL
resource "aws_db_instance" "main" {
  identifier            = "${var.app_name}-${var.environment}"
  engine                = "postgres"
  engine_version        = var.postgres_version
  instance_class        = local.config.rds_instance_class
  allocated_storage     = var.rds_storage_gb
  storage_encrypted     = true
  multi_az              = local.config.rds_multi_az
  publicly_accessible   = false
  skip_final_snapshot   = var.environment == "dev" ? true : false
  deletion_protection   = var.environment == "prod" ? true : false
  backup_retention_days = var.environment == "prod" ? 30 : 7

  db_name  = var.db_name
  username = var.db_username
  password = sensitive(random_password.db.result)

  vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.rds.id]
  db_subnet_group_name   = aws_db_subnet_group.main.name

  depends_on = [aws_db_subnet_group.main]
}

# IAM Role (service account)
resource "aws_iam_role" "service_account" {
  name = "${var.app_name}-service-${var.environment}"

  assume_role_policy = local.config.enable_oidc ? jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Federated = "arn:aws:iam::${data.aws_caller_identity.current.account_id}:oidc-provider/${var.oidc_provider_url}"
        }
        Action = "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity"
        Condition = {
          StringEquals = {
            "${var.oidc_provider_url}:sub" = "system:serviceaccount:default:app-sa"
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  }) : jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          AWS = aws_iam_user.service_account.arn
        }
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
      }
    ]
  })
}

resource "aws_iam_user" "service_account" {
  count = local.config.enable_oidc ? 0 : 1
  name  = "${var.app_name}-service-${var.environment}"
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "app_policy" {
  name   = "${var.app_name}-policy"
  role   = aws_iam_role.service_account.id
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect   = "Allow"
        Action   = local.config.enable_oidc ? ["s3:GetObject", "s3:PutObject"] : ["s3:*"]
        Resource = "${aws_s3_bucket.assets.arn}/*"
      }
    ]
  })
}

# Secrets Manager (DB connection string)
resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret" "db_conn" {
  name = "${var.app_name}/${var.environment}/db-connection"
}

resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret_version" "db_conn" {
  secret_id = aws_secretsmanager_secret.db_conn.id
  secret_string = jsonencode({
    host     = aws_db_instance.main.endpoint
    port     = 5432
    username = var.db_username
    password = random_password.db.result
    dbname   = var.db_name
  })
}
```

---

## Step 2: Module Variables & Outputs

`modules/app/variables.tf`:

```hcl
variable "environment" {
  type        = string
  description = "Environment name (dev or prod)"
  validation {
    condition     = contains(["dev", "prod"], var.environment)
    error_message = "environment must be 'dev' or 'prod'"
  }
}

variable "app_name" {
  type = string
}

variable "region" {
  type = string
}

variable "subnet_ids" {
  type = list(string)
}

variable "kubernetes_version" {
  type    = string
  default = "1.27"
}

variable "postgres_version" {
  type    = string
  default = "14"
}

variable "rds_storage_gb" {
  type    = number
  default = 100
}

variable "db_name" {
  type = string
}

variable "db_username" {
  type = string
}

variable "oidc_provider_url" {
  type    = string
  default = ""
}
```

`modules/app/outputs.tf`:

```hcl
output "eks_cluster_name" {
  value = aws_eks_cluster.main.name
}

output "rds_endpoint" {
  value = aws_db_instance.main.endpoint
}

output "s3_bucket_name" {
  value = aws_s3_bucket.assets.id
}

output "service_role_arn" {
  value = aws_iam_role.service_account.arn
}
```

---

## Step 3: Root Module with `for_each`

`main.tf`:

```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.3"
  required_providers {
    aws = ">= 5.0"
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = var.aws_region
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "west"
  region = "us-west-2"
}

locals {
  environments = {
    dev = {
      region = var.aws_region
    }
    prod = {
      region = var.aws_region
    }
  }
}

module "app" {
  for_each = local.environments

  source = "./modules/app"

  environment        = each.key
  app_name           = var.app_name
  region             = each.value.region
  subnet_ids         = var.subnet_ids[each.key]
  db_name            = var.db_name
  db_username        = var.db_username
  oidc_provider_url  = each.key == "prod" ? var.prod_oidc_provider_url : ""
}

output "clusters" {
  value = {
    for env, mod in module.app : env => mod.eks_cluster_name
  }
}

output "databases" {
  value = {
    for env, mod in module.app : env => mod.rds_endpoint
  }
}
```

---

## Step 4: State Migration (Zero-Diff)

Assuming your current state has separate resources:

```bash
# Before migration, back up your state
terraform state pull > terraform.tfstate.backup

# Migrate each resource to the new module paths
terraform state mv 'aws_eks_cluster.dev' 'module.app["dev"].aws_eks_cluster.main'
terraform state mv 'aws_eks_cluster.prod' 'module.app["prod"].aws_eks_cluster.main'

terraform state mv 'aws_eks_node_group.dev' 'module.app["dev"].aws_eks_node_group.main'
terraform state mv 'aws_eks_node_group.prod' 'module.app["prod"].aws_eks_node_group.main'

terraform state mv 'aws_s3_bucket.dev' 'module.app["dev"].aws_s3_bucket.assets'
terraform state mv 'aws_s3_bucket.prod' 'module.app["prod"].aws_s3_bucket.assets'

# (repeat for all resources: RDS, IAM, security groups, etc.)

# Verify zero-diff
terraform plan
# Should output: Plan: 0 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy.
```

---

## Key Benefits

| Aspect | Benefit |
|---|---|
| **DRY** | Single source of truth for resource logic; environment differences parameterized |
| **Maintainability** | Bug fix applies to both envs immediately |
| **Isolation** | Each environment state is independent; `terraform apply -target=module.app["prod"]` only affects prod |
| **Scalability** | Adding `staging`? Add `"staging": {...}` to `local.environments` |
| **Safety** | State `mv` is non-destructive; resources never recreated |

---

## Conditional Resources (Prod-Only Features)

The module uses `count` for environment-specific resources (e.g., S3 replication, OIDC trust):

```hcl
resource "aws_s3_bucket_replication_configuration" "assets" {
  count = local.config.enable_replication ? 1 : 0  # prod only
  # ...
}
```

When migrating, these conditionals align with your current state automatically—no manual `state mv` needed for `count` resources that don't exist in dev.

---

## Apply & Validate

```bash
terraform init
terraform plan
# Review: Plan: 0 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy.

terraform apply
# (creates no new resources; confirms state alignment)
```

All subsequent deploys reference resources via `module.app["dev"]` and `module.app["prod"]` addresses.
